{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"subset subset","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","fileName":"Subset.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Subset","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 00:29\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations]] · [[Set Theory and Relations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations","Set Theory and Relations","Power Set","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"subset subset","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","fileName":"Subset.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Subset","heading":"Subset","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A subset is a set whose every element belongs to another set. The notation A ⊆ B allows equality, while a proper subset excludes equality.\n\nSubsets become algebraic substructures only when they also preserve the operations of the larger structure.\n\nThe empty set is a subset of every set because an implication beginning with membership in the empty set can have no counterexample. The family of all subsets of A forms its [[Power Set]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Set Relations and Algebraic Foundations","Set Theory and Relations","Power Set","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"subset subset","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Subset.md","fileName":"Subset.md","folder":"6 - Ful